  "diagnosis": "E128 correctly removes opcode-123 socket exchanges, but it retains one synchronous descriptor handoff for each allocation. The dedicated Bionic worker and futex completion path averaged 261.7 microseconds per transaction versus E127's 190.7-microsecond socket transaction. With roughly 531 allocations per present, descriptor blocking rose to 141.0 ms and total measured bridge wait returned to 267.9 ms. The game-authored average consequently regressed from 2.3 to 2.0 FPS. Transport substitution alone is rejected as a performance solution.",
  "recommendation": "Instrument the Bionic worker to split context-mutex wait, journal replay, native vkAllocateDescriptorSets, response encoding, and completion wake. Then virtualize descriptor-pool reset epochs so already-created native descriptor sets can be safely recycled after reset instead of invoking hundreds of real native allocations per frame. Keep E128 available as a diagnostic A/B path, but do not claim it as a speed improvement.",
  "claim_boundary": "This is one complete, thermally cool, matched E128 benchmark. It proves the transport replacement, the regression, and the absence of PRoot from the live game's parent chain. It is not a repeated paired series and does not yet separate native allocation time from worker scheduling time.",
  "reuse": "The required deja query returned no prior descriptor completion-ring implementation. E128 reused E014/E042 shared-memory setup, E075/E075a immutable sequence/failure semantics, E126 canonical journal records, E127 typed allocation outcomes, and the matched 32-present E125-E127 profiling method."
